  summary: "Proposed by Leo Breiman, the Rashomon effect in statistics refers to the phenomenon where multiple different models can achieve similar levels of predictive accuracy on the same dataset. This highlights the ambiguity in model selection and the importance of considering interpretability alongside performance. It challenges the assumption that a single 'best' model exists for complex data.",
